  {
   "t": "exhibits",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "That solved one problem. But it created another. A blunt knife can *cut*, but it can't *spear*. Somebody else had to do the spearing.",
   "p": {
    "tag": "A NEW PROBLEM",
    "word": "Who spears?",
    "items": [
     {
      "obj": "knifeRound",
      "meta": "THE KNIFE",
      "name": "Cuts",
      "sub": "no point",
      "on": "cut"
     },
     {
      "obj": "fork2",
      "meta": "THE FORK",
      "name": "Spears",
      "sub": "two prongs",
      "on": "spear"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "t": "exhibits",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "Therefore, the fork had to grow up. Two prongs weren't enough to hold food steady, so a *third* was added. And in the eighteenth century, in Germany, a curved fork with *four* prongs appeared.",
   "p": {
    "tag": "THE FORK GROWS UP",
    "word": "More prongs.",
    "items": [
     {
      "obj": "fork2",
      "meta": "TWO PRONGS",
      "name": "Two",
      "sub": "medieval",
      "on": "Two"
     },
     {
      "obj": "fork3",
      "meta": "THREE PRONGS",
      "name": "Three",
      "sub": "early 1700s",
      "on": "third"
     },
     {
      "obj": "fork",
      "meta": "FOUR PRONGS",
      "name": "Four",
      "sub": "Germany, 1700s",
      "on": "four"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "t": "today",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "That's the shape of the fork you probably used *today*. It's only about two hundred and fifty years *old*.",
   "p": {}
  },
  {
   "t": "exhibits",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "And here's where the story gets *strange*. In the nineteen seventies, an American anthropologist named Charles Loring *Brace* was studying human teeth.",
   "p": {
    "tag": "THE STRANGEST CONSEQUENCE",
    "word": "Your teeth.",
    "items": [
     {
      "obj": "biteOver",
      "meta": "C. L. BRACE",
      "name": "Human teeth",
      "sub": "1970s research",
      "on": "teeth"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "t": "exhibits",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "He noticed that most people today have an *overbite*: the top front teeth sit in front of the bottom ones. But in Europe, skulls older than a few centuries mostly *don't*.",
   "p": {
    "tag": "WHAT BRACE NOTICED",
    "word": "Two bites.",
    "items": [
     {
      "obj": "biteOver",
      "meta": "MOST PEOPLE TODAY",
      "name": "Overbite",
      "sub": "top in front",
      "on": "overbite"
     },
     {
      "obj": "biteEdge",
      "meta": "OLDER EUROPEAN SKULLS",
      "name": "Edge to edge",
      "sub": "mostly",
      "on": "don't"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "t": "exhibits",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "His explanation was this. Before knives and forks, people often *clamped* a piece of meat in their front teeth and sliced off a mouthful.",
   "p": {
    "tag": "BRACE'S EXPLANATION",
    "word": "Clamp and slice.",
    "items": [
     {
      "obj": "biteEdge",
      "meta": "THE GRIP",
      "name": "Clamp",
      "sub": "front teeth",
      "on": "clamped"
     },
     {
      "obj": "knife",
      "meta": "THE CUT",
      "name": "Slice",
      "sub": "at the lips",
      "on": "sliced"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "t": "exhibits",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "That habit kept the teeth meeting *edge* to edge. Once Europeans began cutting food into small pieces on the *plate*, the front teeth no longer had to grip. Over generations of childhoods, the bite *changed*.",
   "p": {
    "tag": "BRACE'S EXPLANATION",
    "word": "The bite changes.",
    "items": [
     {
      "obj": "biteEdge",
      "meta": "BEFORE",
      "name": "Edge to edge",
      "sub": "teeth grip",
      "on": "edge"
     },
     {
      "obj": "fork",
      "meta": "THEN",
      "name": "Cut on the plate",
      "sub": "knife and fork",
      "on": "plate"
     },
     {
      "obj": "biteOver",
      "meta": "AFTER",
      "name": "Overbite",
      "sub": "no grip needed",
      "on": "changed"
     }
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "t": "map",
   "in": "dolly",
   "pillar": "eat",
   "say": "And in *China*, Brace said he found the same overbite much earlier, around a thousand years ago, in a culture that had been eating pre-cut food with *chopsticks* for centuries.",
   "p": {
    "tag": "CHINA · SONG ERA",
    "title": "Earlier still",
    "cats": {},
    "legend": [],
    "pins": [
     {
      "lonlat": [
